  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1...A gradual warming trend will continue, with a few \nspotty showers and thunderstorms possible Saturday and Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nDry weather will continue to prevail tonight through Saturday \nmorning as surface high pressure remains in place from the eastern \nGreat Lakes to New England. Expect patchy river valley for across \nthe Southern Tier overnight through Saturday morning with limited \nareal extent.\n\nA weak mid level trough will drift into the eastern Great Lakes \nSaturday afternoon. Moisture will be very limited, but the weak \nsynoptic scale ascent interacting with the Lake Erie lake breeze \nboundary may allow for a few isolated showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop Saturday afternoon and evening. The most probable location \nwould be from the western Southern Tier into southern Erie and \nWyoming counties, with dry weather prevailing elsewhere.\n\nThe small amount of convection that develops Saturday afternoon will \nmostly end around sunset Saturday evening. Another, somewhat \nstronger shortwave will move southeast across the Great Lakes and \nreach Western NY Sunday afternoon. The shortwave will again interact \nwith local lake breeze boundaries to support isolated to widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms Sunday afternoon and evening. \nExpect coverage to be sparse with plenty of rain free time.\n\nTemperatures will continue to trend gradually upward in a weak warm \nadvection regime. Highs Saturday will reach the low to mid 80s for \nlower elevations away from the immediate lakeshores, with similar \nhigh temperatures Sunday. Dewpoints will begin to creep upward with \nmodest humidity by Sunday.\n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather will return Monday and Tuesday \nwith increasing coverage of showers and thunderstorms. \n\nA strong mid/upper level ridge will remain centered on the 4-corners \nregion much of next week. A series of Pacific origin shortwaves will \ntraverse the top of the ridge, maintaining a longwave trough \nposition over eastern Canada and New England. The northwest flow \nacross the Great Lakes will maintain a chance of a few showers and \nthunderstorms at times through at least the first half of next week. \n\nLatest model guidance continues to suggest the Monday afternoon \nthrough Tuesday night time frame will feature the best coverage of \nshowers and thunderstorms as a stronger trough and associated cold \nfront cross the eastern Great Lakes. If the timing of stronger \nforcing aligns with the diurnal cycle there may be at least some \nsevere weather potential on Monday and/or Tuesday, but there is \nstill a great deal of uncertainty with the timing details. The \neastern Canada trough is forecast to move east and de-amplify later \nnext week, with a trend towards drier weather for the second half of \nthe week.",
